   ],
   "id": "e7",
   "lines": [
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.8,
      498.48,
      287.76,
      507.12
     ],
     "text": "\u2018Impact\u2019 is a \u2018Salinas\u2019 type of crisphead lettuce resistant to"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.6,
      509.52,
      287.52,
      518.16
     ],
     "text": "known isolates of Bremia lactucae in Australia, possessing the"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.36,
      520.8,
      287.52,
      529.2
     ],
     "text": "Dm genes | and 3 for resistance to Bremia. It is a medium"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.08,
      531.6,
      287.28,
      540.72
     ],
     "text": "maturing variety producing a firm, round, well-covered head."
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.6,
      542.64,
      287.28,
      551.52
     ],
     "text": "There is no anthocyanin in the foliage; wrapper leaves are dark-"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.6,
      553.68,
      287.28,
      562.08
     ],
     "text": "green; at the 34 true leaf stage leaves are erect, lobed, narrow"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.6,
      564.48,
      242.64,
      573.12
     ],
     "text": "elliptical and medium to dark green; seed is white."
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.84,
      581.76,
      78.72,
      590.88
     ],
     "text": "Origin"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.36,
      595.2,
      287.04,
      603.6
     ],
     "text": "The breeder is Mr Dan Trimboli of Arthur Yates & Co. Pty Ltd."
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.32,
      606.0,
      287.28,
      614.4
     ],
     "text": "\u2018Impact\u2019 arose from the controlled pollination of \u2018Jackpot\u2019 by"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.32,
      616.8,
      287.28,
      625.44
     ],
     "text": "\u2018Narromar\u2019. The resultant progeny was backcrossed 5 times to"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.32,
      627.6,
      287.28,
      636.48
     ],
     "text": "\u2018Jackpot\u2019. Prior to backcrossing on each occasion, the progeny"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.6,
      638.88,
      288.0,
      645.6
     ],
     "text": "was screened in vitro for resistance to an Australian isolate of"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.12,
      649.68,
      286.8,
      658.56
     ],
     "text": "Bremia lactucae. Subsequent to the completion of backcross-"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.12,
      660.72,
      286.8,
      669.36
     ],
     "text": "ing, 10 resistant plants were selfed in October 1988. From these"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      52.32,
      671.76,
      287.04,
      680.4
     ],
     "text": "14 resistant plants were again selfed and the progeny screened"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.12,
      682.56,
      286.8,
      691.2
     ],
     "text": "for homozygosity for resistance to mildew. Field nurseries"
    },
    {
     "bbox": [
      51.36,
      693.36,
      286.56,
      702.24
     ],
     "text": "identified phenotypically acceptable plants over two seasons."
    }
   ],
   "page": 1,
   "source": "tesseract-psm3",
   "text": "\u2018Impact\u2019 is a \u2018Salinas\u2019 type of crisphead lettuce resistant to\nknown isolates of Bremia lactucae in Australia, possessing the\nDm genes | and 3 for resistance to Bremia. It is a medium\nmaturing variety producing a firm, round, well-covered head.\nThere is no anthocyanin in the foliage; wrapper leaves are dark-\ngreen; at the 34 true leaf stage leaves are erect, lobed, narrow\nelliptical and medium to dark green; seed is white.\nOrigin\nThe breeder is Mr Dan Trimboli of Arthur Yates & Co. Pty Ltd.\n\u2018Impact\u2019 arose from the controlled pollination of \u2018Jackpot\u2019 by\n\u2018Narromar\u2019. The resultant progeny was backcrossed 5 times to\n\u2018Jackpot\u2019. Prior to backcrossing on each occasion, the progeny\nwas screened in vitro for resistance to an Australian isolate of\nBremia lactucae. Subsequent to the completion of backcross-\ning, 10 resistant plants were selfed in October 1988. From these\n14 resistant plants were again selfed and the progeny screened\nfor homozygosity for resistance to mildew. Field nurseries\nidentified phenotypically acceptable plants over two seasons.",
   "text_format": "plain",
   "type": "text"
  },
